<attribute name="usageGuidance" nodeId="864863d0"></attribute>
<attribute name="refinedDescription" nodeId="aabccf0e">&lt;p&gt;
    The Definition of Done is a list of conditions that an item of work must meet before it is deemed complete. Conditions
    included in the Definition of Done are applicable either to all work items (i.e., entire Product Backlog) or a subset
    of work items (i.e. individual Backlog Items). In the event there are material differences in the outputs of various
    work items, be sure to develop conditions&amp;nbsp; specific to a related subset of&amp;nbsp;work&amp;nbsp;as opposed to using a
    generic set across all work items (i.e., the&amp;nbsp;entire Product Backlog).
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Conditions included in the Definition of Done are typically supplemented by additional criteria specific to an
    individual&amp;nbsp;component of work&amp;nbsp;(i.e., acceptance criteria for a specific Backlog Item).
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    All members of the team must have a shared understanding of the conditions to provide transparency and product quality.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Definition of Done typically includes criteria, which oblige the Development Team to:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Create agreed documentation
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Meet non-functional requirements (mainly applies to technical deliveries)
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Test, as needed (mainly applies to technical deliveries)
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Store code or documents
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Obtain peer or Product Owner review
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Meet defined acceptance criteria
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    The Definition of Done is not a static list. As the team and stakeholders learn more about the product, as it is
    created, or the creation process itself, they may update the criteria. It is important that revisions to the Definition
    of Done are implemented prior to the start of a sprint as the pace of work would be disturbed if introduced during
    in-flight sprints.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
</Element>
